diff --git a/docs/parameters.md b/docs/parameters.md
index 58f3219..9ad4a3e 100644
--- a/docs/parameters.md
+++ b/docs/parameters.md
@@ -53,3 +53,15 @@ The option's names are `-t` and `--times`. More names are available for options
 
     invoke(multi_echo, ['--times=3', 'index.txt'], prog_name='multi_echo')
 ```
+
+### Parameter Spec
+
+{attr}`Parameter.spec` is how the user spells the parameter on the command line. For an option, it is the declaration with the longest prefix, so `-t, --times` gives `--times`, and `--color/--no-color` gives `--color`. Metavars are left out, and a secondary spelling is used only when there is no primary declaration. For an argument, it is the `metavar` if one was given, otherwise the upper-cased name, so `click.argument("filename")` gives `FILENAME`.
+
+Click reads `spec` in only a few places: the deprecation warnings for options and arguments, the `ValueError` raised for a parameter that is both required and deprecated, the `MissingParameter` message when no custom message is given, and `Argument.get_error_hint()` when there is no context. Usage errors for options take their hint from `opts` instead, and usage errors for arguments with a context take it from {meth}`Parameter.make_metavar`. Overriding `spec` in a subclass only changes those places.
+
+{meth}`Parameter.get_help_spec` returns the same value. {meth}`Option.get_help_spec` returns the full left help column, such as `-t, --times INTEGER`, and {meth}`Argument.get_help_spec` returns the usage metavar, such as `[FILENAME]...`.
+
+```{versionadded} 8.6
+`spec` and `Parameter.get_help_spec()`. `Parameter.human_readable_name` is deprecated in favor of `spec`.
+```
